QB.add(2,[{"n":1,"q":"A 52-year-old male presents with worsening shortness of breath and fatigue. An echocardiogram shows left ventricular dilation and significant aortic regurgitation. Which of the following are the possible conditions causing Aortic regurgitation? Ankylosing Spondylitis Aortic Dissection Marfan's Syndrome Syphilis","o":[{"t":"1,2,3"},{"t":"2,3,4"},{"t":"1,3,4"},{"t":"1,2,3,4"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"All of the above are potential causes of aortic regurgitation but through different mechanisms."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Aortic Regurgitation"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Aetiology Conditions affecting the valve Conditions causing valve"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"structure and its functions root dilatation"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Bicuspid Aortic valve Aortic Dissection (statement 2) (Congenital) Aortitis (Takayasu arteritis or GCA) Infective Endocarditis Hypertension Rheumatic fever Marfan syndrome (statement 3) Myxomatous prolapse of the Medial Degeneration valve Nonsyndromic familial aneurysm Scarring of the valve by Syphilis (statement 4) Ankylosing spondylitis (statement 1) Radiation Trauma"},{"k":"img","v":3430},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Pathophysiology Aortic Regurgitation"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Clinical symptoms Usually, it starts appearing only"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"after 10 to 15 years. Symptoms specific to AR : Palpitations (more in the supine position) Head pounding sensation Other symptoms: Paroxysmal Nocturnal dyspnea (sometimes the early one to appear) Angina on rest or exertion & breathlessness on exertion Orthopnea and diaphoresis Peripheral edema"}]},{"n":2,"q":"Match the following murmurs with the respective conditions. S.No Murmur Condition 1) a) Mitral Stenosis 2) b) Aortic Stenosis 3) c) Mitral Regurgitation 4) d) Aortic Regurgitation\nS.No Murmur Condition\n1) a) Mitral Stenosis 2) b) Aortic Stenosis\n3) c) Mitral Regurgitation\n4) d) Aortic Regurgitation","o":[{"t":"1-b, 2-d, 3-a, 4-c"},{"t":"1-c, 2-a, 3-d, 4-b"},{"t":"1-a, 2-c, 3-b, 4-d"},{"t":"1-d, 2-b, 3-c, 4-a"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"img","v":3433},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Aortic Stenosis: Crescendo-decrescendo ejection murmur"},{"k":"img","v":3435},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Mitral/Tricuspid Regurgitation: Holosystolic, high-pitched “blowing” murmur"},{"k":"img","v":3433},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Aortic Stenosis: Crescendo-decrescendo ejection murmur"},{"k":"img","v":3438},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Aortic Regurgitation: Early diastolic, decrescendo, high-pitched “blowing” murmur"},{"k":"img","v":3440},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Mitral Stenosis: Delayed rumbling mid-to-late diastolic murmur with opening snap"},{"k":"img","v":3433},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Aortic Stenosis: Crescendo-decrescendo ejection murmur"},{"k":"img","v":3443},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Mitral valve prolapse: Late crescendo murmur with mid systolic click (MC) that occurs after carotid pulse"},{"k":"img","v":3445},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Ventricular septal defect: Holosystolic, harsh-sounding murmur"}],"qi":[3401,3404,3406,3408]},{"n":3,"q":"A 25 y/o patient with h/o pulmonary hypertension presents with exertional dyspnea. You auscultate a high-pitched, decrescendo diastolic murmur at the second left intercostal space, radiating along the left sternal border that increases with inspiration. Which of the following murmurs is this?","o":[{"t":"Graham Steell murmur"},{"t":"Austin Flint murmur"},{"t":"Carey-Coombs murmur"},{"t":"Gibson’s murmur"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The clinical scenario of a patient with h/o pulmonary hypertension & a high-pitched, decrescendo diastolic murmur heard at the second left intercostal space, radiating along the left sternal border that becomes louder with inspiration indicates a Graham steel murmur."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Graham Steel Murmur:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"A high-pitched, decrescendo diastolic murmur is best heard at the second left intercostal space and radiates along the left sternal border."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Often associated with pulmonary regurgitation (PR) , particularly in patients with significant pulmonary artery (PA) hypertension ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It can become louder with inspiration."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Accompanied by a loud and sometimes palpable P2 and right ventricular (RV) lift."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"pulmonary regurgitation\" data-author=\"\" data-hash=\"8873\" data-license=\"\" data-source=\"\" data-tags=\"April2025\" height=\"494\" src=\"https://image.prepladder.com/notes/0tgpYHQephSIFpU KaWP41744312057.png\" width=\"494\" />"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Austin Flint Murmur (Option B) :"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Soft, low-pitched, rumbling mid-to-late diastolic murmur produced by the diastolic displacement of the anterior leaflet of the mitral valve due to the aortic regurgitation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Not indicative of hemodynamically significant mitral valve obstruction."},{"k":"img","v":3448},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Carey-Coombs Murmur (Option C) :"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Mid-diastolic murmur is commonly heard in cases of acute rheumatic fever , likely due to flow through an edematous mitral valve"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Disappears with resolution of the acute episode."},{"k":"img","v":3451},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Gibson murmur (Option D): It is a continuous, rumbling heart murmur that's usually heard in the second left interspace near the sternum & often seen in patent ductus arteriosus (PDA)"},{"k":"img","v":3453}]},{"n":4,"q":"A 65-year-old male presents to the clinic with complaints of left arm pain and intermittent dizziness. O/E, you find a notable difference in blood pressure readings: 150/90 mmHg in the right arm and 100/60 mmHg in the left arm. Which condition is least likely to cause this variation?","o":[{"t":"Coarctation of the aorta"},{"t":"Aortic dissection"},{"t":"Supravalvular aortic stenosis"},{"t":"Takayasu arteritis"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Supravalvular aortic stenosis can affect blood flow, but it usually does not create significant differences in blood pressure between the arms, as both arms receive blood from the same arch branches. Therefore, supravalvular aortic stenosis is least likely to cause this variation."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Coarctation of the Aorta (CoA) (Option A) typically causes lower blood pressure in the lower extremities compared to the upper extremities, but can also lead to differences between arms due to collateral circulation."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Aortic Dissection (Option B) can cause differences in blood pressure between arms if the dissection extends into the subclavian arteries or affects the branches that supply the arms."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Takayasu Arteritis (Option D) is an inflammatory condition that often affects the aorta and its branches and may cause stenosis or occlusion leading to differences in blood pressure between the arms."}]},{"n":5,"q":"Match the following cardiac valvular disorders with their specific murmur type: Valvular defect Murmur 1. Aortic stenosis a. Pansystolic murmur increasing with inspiration 2. Mitral regurgitation b. Mid-diastolic rumble with opening snap 3. Mitral stenosis c. Harsh systolic murmur radiating to the carotids 4. Tricuspid regurgitation d. Holosystolic murmur radiating to the axilla\nValvular defect Murmur\n1. Aortic stenosis a. Pansystolic murmur increasing with inspiration 2. Mitral regurgitation b. Mid-diastolic rumble with opening snap\n3. Mitral stenosis c. Harsh systolic murmur radiating to the carotids\n4. Tricuspid regurgitation d. Holosystolic murmur radiating to the axilla","o":[{"t":"1-b, 2-a, 3-d, 4-c"},{"t":"1-c, 2-b, 3-a, 4-d"},{"t":"1-c, 2-d, 3-b, 4-a"},{"t":"1-d, 2-a, 3-c, 4-b"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Valv Most common ECG findings JVP finding Type of Specific features ular cause s murmur disea se"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Mitral Rheumatic Atrial fibrillation Changes Holosystoli Wide variable regu heart disease may be present are seen c murmur splitting of S2 rgitati Degenerative P-mitrale > 120 only in radiating A2 appears on seconds (enlarg advanced to the axilla. early as less"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"ed left atrium) cases. blood is flowing"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"through the aortic valve; thus, the S2 split is wide"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Mitral Rheumatic Right axis Mid-diastoli Loud S1 sten heart disease deviation c rumble Malar rash osis RV hypertrophy. with an Distended"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"opening neck veins snap."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Tricu Rheumatic Tall P waves in Large ‘a’ Mid-diastoli Carvallo’s sign spid (coexist with the lead II wave c murmur steno mitral stenosi Right axis Blunted ‘y’ sis s) deviation wave Valv Most common ECG findings JVP finding Type of Specific features ular cause s murmur disea se"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Tricu Rheumatic ‘ C-v’ wave Blowing Systolic spid Steep ‘y’ holosystolic liver pulsati regur descent murmur on gitati Carvallo’s on sign"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Aorti Infective Left axis deviatio Changes Decrescen Wide pulse c reg endocarditis n are seen do diastolic pressure urgit Bicuspid LV hypertrophy only in murmur Water hammer ation aortic valve advanced pulse"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"cases"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Aorti Degenerative Accentuated “a” Crescendo- Gallavardin c ste calcification wave due to decrescend effect nosis 53% of cases reduced RV o ejection"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"have congenit cavity distensibili murmur . al bicuspid ty from the aortic valve hypertrophied"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"interventricular septum"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Pulm Pulmonary Right axis Large ‘a’ End–diasto Cardiac MRI is onar hypertension deviation wave lic murmur the IOC rather y reg RV hypertrophy. (Graham than echocardio urgit Steel mur graphy ation mur)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Pulm Congenital Systolic ejection Wide split onar defect murmur S2 y ste nosis"}]},{"n":6,"q":"A 6-year-old girl with a history of Tetralogy of Fallot repair 12 months ago presents with abdominal swelling and pedal oedema. Cardiac auscultation reveals an early diastolic murmur, and ECG shows right ventricular and atrial enlargement. Considering the suspicion of pulmonary regurgitation, which investigation is most appropriate for further evaluation?","o":[{"t":"Transthoracic echocardiography"},{"t":"Cardiac magnetic resonance imaging"},{"t":"Transesophageal echocardiography"},{"t":"Cardiac catheterisation"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Cardiac MRI is the investigation of choice to diagnose pulmonary regurgitation , especially in patients with repaired congenital heart defects , as it provides more anatomical details and helps precisely access the Right ventricular volume and function."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"PULMONARY REGURGITATION"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Aetiology Pulmonary artery hypertension - annular dilatation of the"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"pulmonary valve Post-repair of Tetralogy of Fallot Post balloon valvotomy Marfan syndrome"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Pathophysiology Severe pulmonary regurgitation (PR) results in right"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"ventricular enlargement and eccentric hypertrophy due to increased preload and afterload. A reverse pressure gradient from the pulmonary artery to the right ventricle drives the regurgitation, leading to a reduction in cardiac output over time. As the condition progresses, right atrial involvement may occur, elevating jugular venous pressure."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Clinical features Fatigue, dyspnea, ascites, and pedal oedema"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Auscultation reveals an early diastolic murmur (Graham steel murmur)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Investigations Chest X-ray: Enlarged right atrium and ventricular"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"shadow Cardiac MRI: Investigation of choice, especially in patients with repaired congenital heart defects. Echocardiography (transthoracic): It helps assess pulmonary valve morphology and the direction of blood flow. (Option A and C) Cardiac catheterisation is not usually done but can be done during transcatheter pulmonary valve procedures. (Option D)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Treatment Medical: Diuretics for symptoms of right-sided heart"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"failure Surgical: Transcatheter pulmonic valve replacement."}]},{"n":7,"q":"A 10-year-old girl presents with exercise intolerance and occasional chest pain. Examination reveals a systolic ejection murmur at the left upper sternal border and elevated jugular venous pressure. An echocardiogram shows a peak systolic gradient of 70 mmHg across the pulmonary valve, raising suspicion for pulmonary valve stenosis. Which of the following set of statements is true regarding this condition? Pulmonic stenosis is always congenital. Dysplastic valves leading to pulmonary stenosis are seen in Noonan syndrome. Severe PS is defined by peak systolic gradient across the PV>64 mmHg Percutaneous balloon valvuloplasty is indicated for all patients. Pulmonic stenosis usually leads to right ventricular hypertrophy.","o":[{"t":"2 and 5"},{"t":"3 and 4"},{"t":"2,3 and 5"},{"t":"1,2 and 5"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Pulmonary stenosis can involve dysplastic valves in Noonan syndrome. It is considered severe when the peak systolic gradient exceeds 64 mmHg and typically leads to right ventricular hypertrophy due to pressure overload."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"PULMONARY STENOSIS"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Aetiology It is essentially a congenital disease, but it may be"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"acquired (Statement 1 ruled out) Noonan syndrome (dysplastic pulmonary valve) (Statement 2) Tetralogy of Fallot Carcinoids Bulky vegetation or tumours"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Pathophysiology Pulmonary stenosis obstructs blood flow from the right"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"ventricle, resulting in increased chamber pressure and subsequent right ventricular hypertrophy. (Statement 5) Right ventricular dysfunction occurs early and at lower pressures due to its reduced adaptability to hemodynamic burden compared to the left ventricle."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Severity Defined by the systolic pressure gradient between the"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"RV and PA Severe: Peak systolic gradient >64 mm hg (Statement 3) Moderate: Peak systolic gradient 36-64 mm hg Mild: Peak systolic gradient <36 hh mg"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Clinical features Mild to moderate PS are asymptomatic."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Severe PS: Exertional dyspnea, fatigue, anginal pain and systemic venous congestion Syncope may occur during stress. Auscultation: Mid-systolic, crescendo-decrescendo murmur preceded by an ejection click. JVP: Prominent ‘a’ wave"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Investigations ECG: Right axis deviation + Right ventricular hypertrophy"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Chest X-ray: Post-stenotic dilation of the main PA and RV enlargement. Echocardiography: Transthoracic : Helps assess jet velocity, gradients, ventricular function Transesophageal (for detailed assessment of RV outflow tract)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Management Medical: Diuretics for symptoms of right-sided heart"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"failure Surgical: Percutaneous pulmonary balloon valvuloplasty (Statement 4 ruled out) is done when Symptomatic patients with moderate to severe PS Asymptomatic patients with peak gradient >64 mmHg or mean gradient > 35 mmHg."}]},{"n":8,"q":"A 75-year-old male with a history of long-standing hypertension and atrial fibrillation presents with progressive fatigue & bilateral leg swelling. O/E, the patient has distended neck veins with the following waveform with a blowing holosystolic murmur that increases with inspiration. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Pulmonary hypertension"},{"t":"Tricuspid regurgitation"},{"t":"Pulmonary stenosis"},{"t":"Mitral regurgitation"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This is likely tricuspid regurgitation , as the patient presents with jugular venous distension (prominent C-V waves, rapid Y descent) , ascites, and hepatomegaly."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The characteristic finding is a blowing holosystolic murmur at the lower left sternal border, which increases with inspiration."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"TRICUSPID REGURGI TATION"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Aetiology Primary (organic)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Rheumatic disease Infective endocarditis Myxomatous - tricuspid valve prolapse Carcinoid Radiation Congenital (Ebstein’s anomaly) Trauma Myocardial infarction (Papillary muscle rupture) Secondary (functional) Long-standing pulmonary hypertension Left-sided heart disease Cardiomyopathy Atrial fibrillation - functional tricuspid regurgitation Chronic RV apical pacing (dyssynchrony)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Pathophysiology An incompetent tricuspid valve allows blood to flow back"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"from the right ventricle (RV) into the right atrium (RA) during systole, increasing RA and jugular venous pressures, seen as prominent C-V waves . Severe TR causes ventricularization of the RA waveform and RA dilation, resulting in reduced cardiac output that does not improve with exercise."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"TRICUSPID REGURGI TATION"},{"k":"img","v":3413},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Clinical features"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Features of right-sided heart failure Distended neck veins Hepatomegaly Ascites Edema Positive hepatojugular reflex Pulsatile liver (Systolic pulsation) Auscultation: Blowing holosystolic murmur along the lower left sternal border, which intensifies with inspiration (Carvallo’s sign)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Investigations Chest X-ray : Right atrial and right ventricular"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"enlargement seen. ECG : Tall P waves in lead II (RA enlargement) and Right axis deviation. Echocardiogram (Gold standard) Right atrial and ventricular dilation. Tricuspid valve annular dilation. Regurgitant jet from RV to RA on Doppler imaging."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Management Medical therapy :"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Diuretics are used to manage the symptoms of right-sided heart failure Aldosterone antagonists may be added in cases of significant volume overload. Surgical intervention : Tricuspid valve repair or replacement may be considered for patients with severe TR"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Pulmonary hypertension (Option A) can cause right-sided heart failure, but it does not typically present with a holosystolic murmur or intensification of the murmur with inspiration. Instead, the primary finding is a loud P2 sound due to increased pressure in the pulmonary arteries."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Pulmonary stenosis (Option C) presents with an ejection systolic murmur heard best over the pulmonic area, not a holosystolic murmur, and does not typically intensify with inspiration."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Mitral regurgitation (Option D) causes a holosystolic murmur , which is best heard at the apex and radiates to the axilla. As it involves the left side of the heart, mitral regurgitation does not intensify with inspiration, and the jugular venous findings are not typical of left-sided heart disease."}],"qi":[3413]},{"n":9,"q":"A 66-year-old female presents with fatigue, abdominal discomfort, and swelling in her legs. On examination, she has jugular venous distention with giant ‘a’ waves, hepatomegaly, and ascites. Auscultation reveals a diastolic murmur at the lower left sternal border, which increases in intensity with inspiration. What’s the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Mitral stenosis"},{"t":"Tricuspid stenosis"},{"t":"Constrictive pericarditis"},{"t":"Aortic stenosis"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The diastolic murmur at the lower left sternal border that increases with inspiration, along with jugular venous distention with giant ‘a’ waves , hepatomegaly , and ascites, is suggestive of Tricuspid stenosis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This condition is characterised by restricted blood flow from the right atrium to the right ventricle, often leading to right-sided heart failure symptoms like swelling in the legs, abdominal discomfort, and hepatomegaly."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The presence of a diastolic murmur at the lower left sternal border further supports the diagnosis."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"TRICUSPID STENOSIS"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Aetiology Congenital"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Rheumatic origin (often coexists with mitral stenosis)"},{"k":"img","v":3462},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Pathophysiology"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"RA pressure › , due to the diastolic pressure gradient between RA and RV, results in systemic venous congestion. Restricted forward flow of blood leads to fl cardiac output."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"TRICUSPID STENOSIS"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Clinical features Fatigue"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Mild dyspnea Venous congestion (right-side heart failure): Hepatomegaly, ascites, oedema and may lead to cardiac cirrhosis. Physical examination: Jugular venous distension with prominent ‘a’ waves Auscultation: Mid-diastolic murmur at left lower sternal border Murmur increases with inspiration (Carvallo's sign). If mitral stenosis coexist Pulmonary artery hypertension Orthopnea due to pulmonary oedema"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Investigations Chest X-ray and ECG show features of RA enlargement"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Echocardiogram: Thickened tricuspid valve with diastolic doming and increased pressure gradient between RV and RA."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Management Medical management: To reduce systemic venous"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"congestion symptoms Dietary salt restriction Loop diuretics Surgical intervention: Valvular repair or Prosthetic valve replacement."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Mitral stenosis (MS) (Option A) causes left atrial pressure overload , leading to pulmonary congestion and dyspnea, often with orthopnea and paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea . This patient’s presentation is dominated by right-sided heart failure symptoms (ascites, hepatomegaly, oedema) without the significant pulmonary congestion typically seen in MS."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Constrictive pericarditis (Option C) causes a thickened, non-compliant pericardium, limiting diastolic filling and leading to systemic venous congestion (jugular venous distension, hepatomegaly, ascites). Heart sounds may be muffled, and a pericardial knock can be present. Unlike tricuspid stenosis, constrictive pericarditis lacks a diastolic murmur."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Aortic stenosis (Option D) causes left ventricular outflow obstruction, leading to exertional dyspnea, angina, and syncope, with a systolic ejection murmur radiating to the carotids. This patient's right-sided heart failure and diastolic murmur are more consistent with tricuspid stenosis, not aortic stenosis."}]},{"n":10,"q":"A 69-year-old man presents with severe aortic regurgitation. He reports increasing fatigue, breathlessness, and episodes of dizziness. On auscultation, a diastolic murmur is heard along with a water-hammer pulse, and an echocardiogram shows significant left ventricular dilation and an ejection fraction of 55%. What is the most appropriate management for this patient?","o":[{"t":"Medical management"},{"t":"Transcatheter aortic valve implantation"},{"t":"Intra-aortic balloon counterpulsation"},{"t":"Aortic valve replacement"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Aortic valve replacement is the ideal management for severe aortic regurgitation patients who are symptomatic, irrespective of their LV function."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In asymptomatic progressive aortic regurgitation , AVR is advisable if LV dysfunction is evident."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Regular non-invasive follow-up, once in 6-12 months, is necessary to monitor the development of myocardial dysfunction , as surgery delayed long after its development has minimal chances of completing LV recovery."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Work up for Aortic regurgitation"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Investigations Findings"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"ECG LV Hypertrophy"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Left axis deviation is noted QRS prolongation is seen occasionally LV Strain - ST wave depression and T wave inversion in I, aVL, V5, V6 leads"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Chest X-ray Inferolateral displacement of the apex is noted"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Aortic root dilation, often associated with aortic regurgitation, can be visible on a lateral chest X-ray as it fills the retrosternal space. It is not very sensitive to Aortic root conditions"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Echocardiogram Rapid fluttering of the anterior mitral valve during"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"diastole because of the regurgitation. A dilated left ventricle is noted. It is the investigation of choice in Severe AR"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Cardiac Catheterization and To evaluate the LV function and regurgitation Angiography Done before surgery, but CT angiography can be done (Occasionally done) instead."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Man age ment"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Type Acute AR Chronic AR Man age ment"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Medi IV Diuretics (Furosemide) and Diuretics and Vasodilators like cal Vasodilators (Sodium Nitroprusside) ACEi, CCB, (Opti are used to stabilise the patient. Hydralazine, on A) Beta Blockers are contraindicated ARBs (can be used in chronic AR)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"as they worsen the symptoms and to manage HTN; Target SBP <140 reduce the cardiac output. mm/Hg."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"For Angina: Nitrates For Syphilis: Benzathine Penicillin, 2.4 million units IM, three doses given with one week gap."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Surg Aortic valve replacement (AVR) is AVR is indicated when developing ical the treatment of choice, usually LV dysfunction or symptoms ."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"within 24 hours of diagnosis. Transcatheter aortic valve Intra-Aortic Balloon pum p is implantation (Option B) is not contraindicated as it worsens the suggeste d due to its limited regurgitant flow. (Option C) success in chronic conditions."}]},{"n":11,"q":"Match the following signs with specific features Signs Features 1. Muller Sign a) Systolic pulsations of the Spleen 2. Rosenbach sign b) Visible systolic pulsation of the uvula 3. Gerhardt's sign c) Jarring motion of the head with each heartbeat 4. De-Musset sign d) Systolic pulsations of the Liver\nSigns Features 1. Muller Sign a) Systolic pulsations of the Spleen\n2. Rosenbach sign b) Visible systolic pulsation of the uvula\n3. Gerhardt's sign c) Jarring motion of the head with each heartbeat\n4. De-Musset sign d) Systolic pulsations of the Liver","o":[{"t":"1-b, 2-d, 3-a, 4-c"},{"t":"1-a, 2-c, 3-b, 4-d"},{"t":"1-c, 2-a, 3-d, 4-b"},{"t":"1-d, 2-b, 3-c, 4-a"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Signs of Aortic Regurgitation"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Muller Sign It is a visible systolic pulsation of the uvula"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Rosenbach sign Systolic pulsations of the Liver"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Gerhardt's sign Systolic pulsations of the Spleen"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Quincke's sign Repeatedly alternating blushing and blanching of"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"capillaries in the nail folds upon palpation (Capillary Pulsations)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Landolphi sign Alternating systolic constriction and diastolic dilatation of"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"pupils"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Traube Sign It is pistol shot sounds that are heard while auscultating"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"over the femoral artery."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Signs of Aortic Regurgitation"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Water-Hammer pulse Strong pulse that rises quickly and then drops off"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"suddenly."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Duroziez Sign It is a to-and-fro murmur heard while auscultating the"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"femoral artery."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"De-Musset sign The jarring motion of the head with each heartbeat"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Examination Findings of Aortic Regurgitation"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Blood Pressure Elevated systolic pressure and"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"decreased diastolic pressure result in increased arterial pulse pressure . Atrio-ventricular malformation and Atrio-ventricular nstula also show low diastolic BP due to continuous Korotkon sounds. Positive Hill’s sign is an abnormal difference in BP in the upper limb and lower limb."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"On Palpation Heaving LV impulse is noted, which"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"is displaced laterally and inferiorly Diastolic and Systolic Thrills were noticed at the left lower sternal border and suprasternal notch, respectively."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"On Auscultation Heart Sounds A2 is absent"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"In the Bicuspid aortic valve, systolic ejection sound is present Sometimes, A4 is also heard"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Examination Findings of Aortic Regurgitation"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Murmurs A Decrescendo diastolic murmur,"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"best heard when sitting up and leaning forward, is an early diastolic murmur that becomes holo-diastolic as the condition progresses to a severe stage. Musical diastolic murmur is aortic cusp fluttering in the regurgitant flow. Mid-diastolic murmur Austin Flint murmur is a soft, low-pitched, mid- to end-diastolic murmur caused by mitral valve displacement due to AR. Ejection systolic murmur is commonly heard in AR along with AS ."}]},{"n":12,"q":"A 72-year-old male with a two-year history of severe aortic regurgitation presents with complaints of worsening dyspnea, angina and orthopnea. Physical examination reveals a loud diastolic murmur at the left sternal border and a widened pulse pressure. An echocardiogram indicates significant left ventricular dilation. What is the likely estimated regurgitant volume in this condition?","o":[{"t":"< 35 ml/beat"},{"t":"> 35 ml/beat"},{"t":"< 60 ml/beat"},{"t":"> 60 ml/beat"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The typical regurgitant volume in a patient with severe aortic regurgitation exceeds 60 mL per beat."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"An echocardiogram is utilised to detect changes and classify the condition as severe or progressive aortic regurgitation."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Echocardiogram Findings for Aortic Regurgitation"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Severe Aortic regurgitation Progressive Aortic regurgitation"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Regurgitant Volume is > 60 ml/beat Ejection Fraction is 55 - 60 % Regurgitant fraction is ‡50% Left ventricle end-systolic dimension Holodiastolic aortic flow reversal - blood flows (LVESD) is >50 mm backwards into the left ventricle throughout the diastolic Left ventricle end-diastolic dimension phase. (LVEDD) is > 65 mm Diastolic flow reversal in the descending aorta."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Acute Aortic Regurgitation Chronic Aortic Regurgitation"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"It is a sudden onset of significant backflow from the The presence of a water-hammer pulse/Corrigan’s pulse aorta into the left ventricle, causing LV dilatation. is a strong pulse that rises quickly and then drops off This leads to pulmonary oedema, which can suddenly. progress to acute heart failure. Quincke's pulse (Capillary pulsations)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"The Frank-Starling law explains that blood flowing back into the left ventricle increases its contractions due to stretching of the ventricle walls. Reduced Cardiac output at rest. Both Preload and Afterload are increased ( Laplace law) LVH is common. High myocardial oxygen demand can lead to Myocardial ischemia."}]},{"n":13,"q":"A 32-year-old female presents with intermittent palpitations, chest discomfort, and fatigue. A mid-systolic click and late systolic murmur are heard at the apex. Echocardiography shows mitral valve leaflets billowing into the left atrium during systole. Which of the following best explains the pathophysiology of this condition?","o":[{"t":"Inflammation of the mitral valve due to rheumatic fever"},{"t":"Fibrotic changes affecting the mitral valves"},{"t":"Rupture of the tendinae of the mitral valve"},{"t":"Myxomatous degeneration of mitral valve apparatus."}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Myxomatous degeneration of the mitral valve apparatus leads to mitral valve leaflets billowing into the left atrium during systole, thus causing Mitral valve prolapse."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"MITRAL VALVE PROLAPSE"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Aetiology Most cases are idiopathic"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Myxomatous degeneration of the mitral valve apparatus (Reduced type III collagen production) Marfan syndrome (Fibrillin-1 protein defect) Ehler Danlos syndrome Osteogenesis imperfecta Straight back syndrome ( reduced thoracic kyphosis ) compresses the heart between the sternum and vertebrae, altering its position and increasing the likelihood of mitral valve prolapse (MVP)."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Pathophysiology mitral valve prolapse\" data-author=\"\" data-hash=\"8847\""},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"data-license=\"\" data-source=\"\" data-tags=\"April2025\" height=\"230\" src=\"https://image.prepladder.com/notes/z Nqtoziid3ggvNttuHvn1744309432.png\" width=\"462\" />"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"MITRAL VALVE PROLAPSE"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Clinical features Incidence"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"More in females: 15-30 years Males: 50 years Severity: More severe in men than women Symptoms: Initially asymptomatic Palpitation and syncope due to premature ventricular contraction. Atypical features Substernal pain without exertion. Transient ischemic attack. Risk of endocarditis and Sudden cardiac death. If Mitral regurgitation (MR) occurs, it can lead to increased pressure in the lungs, causing frank pulmonary oedema."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Auscultation findings of Mitral valve prolapse"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Mid or late non-ejection systolic click"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"High-pitched mid-late systolic crescendo-decrescendo murmur best heard at the apex."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Change in auscultation findings with change in cavity size"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Condition Valsalva or Sudden Standing Squatting, Leg Raising, Standing to"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Supine, Isometric Exercises"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Preload and Afterload fl ›"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Cavity size fl ›"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Chordae tendineae fl › stretching"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Timing of click The click occurs earlier, closer The click occurs later, away from S1"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"to S1"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Murmur duration › fl"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Inflammation of the mitral valve due to rheumatic fever (Option A) occurs in Rheumatic heart disease, which can result in fibrotic changes in the mitral valve (Option B), leading to mitral stenosis, which produces a low-pitched diastolic rumble."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Rupture of the tendinae of the mitral valve (Option C) leads to acute severe mitral regurgitation, which can be a complication of mitral valve prolapse but is not the cause."}]},{"n":14,"q":"A 65-year-old woman reports experiencing mild chest pain and shortness of breath on exertion. Upon examination, a prominent ejection-type systolic murmur is noted, best heard at the right second intercostal space and radiating to the carotids. An Echocardiogram shows an aortic valve area of 0.9 cm², and ECG reveals Left ventricular hypertrophy. Which stage of aortic stenosis is suspected, and what is the ideal management? (TAVR: Transcatheter Aortic Valve Replacement)","o":[{"t":"Stage B and TAVR"},{"t":"Stage D and Statins"},{"t":"Stage B and Statins"},{"t":"Stage D and TAVR"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Aortic Valve area of < 1 cm² (Severe Stenosis) along with symptoms are categorized into Stage D and is managed by Transcatheter Aortic Valve Replacement (TAVR)."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Stage Aortic Valve Explanation"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"area"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Stage A Normal Patient with normal aortic valve"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"functioning and presence of risk factors like: Old age Congenital valvular disease History of rheumatic fever History of endocarditis Genetic Polymorphisms HTN, DM, CKD, Smoking & High LDL."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Stage B 1.5 - 2 cm² It involves asymptomatic patients"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"(Mild) with mild to moderate stenosis."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"1 - 1.5 cm² (Moderate)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Stage C < 1 cm² Asymptomatic severe aortic stenosis"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Stage D < 1 cm² Symptomatic severe aortic stenosis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Patients with aortic stenosis are typically managed medically unless they are symptomatic, with a few exceptions."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Stage/Condition Management"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Asymptomatic with HTN or CAD Start ACE inhibitors or beta-blockers."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Stage B Consider TAVR if LVEF < 60% (based on three"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"readings). Regular monitoring with Doppler echocardiogram every 1-2 years to evaluate progression."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Stage/Condition Management"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Stage C Avoid strenuous exercise, hypovolemia, and"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"dehydration to maintain adequate cardiac output. Indications for TAVR : LVEF < 50%. Ejection velocity > 5 m/sec. Transvalvular gradient (LV-AO) > 60 mmHg. Statins should be started to prevent the progression of atherosclerosis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Stage D TAVR is indicated."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"For pediatric patients with congenital aortic stenosis, aortic balloon valvuloplasty is the preferred treatment."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Patients in Stage B and Stage C are monitored regularly with Doppler echocardiograms every 1 to 2 years to assess disease progression."},{"k":"img","v":3475}]},{"n":15,"q":"A 72-year-old patient presents with angina and shortness of breath on exertion. Physical examination reveals a slow-rising carotid pulse and a systolic ejection murmur. An echocardiogram shows thickened and calcified aortic valve leaflets with reduced opening. Which of the following findings is most likely to be observed on the ECG in this patient?","o":[{"t":"Left ventricular hypertrophy"},{"t":"Right atrial enlargement"},{"t":"ST-segment elevation"},{"t":"Atrial fibrillation"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The presence of thickened and calcified aortic valve leaflets, leading to reduced opening, indicates Aortic stenosis (AS)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In this condition, the heart works harder to pump blood through the narrowed valve, leading to left ventricular hypertrophy (LVH), which is frequently observed on ECG due to the increased workload."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Physical Findings:"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Heart Rate Generally regular until late stages."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Atrial fibrillation (AF) may indicate associated mitral valve disease"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Blood Press It is commonly elevated in older adults with aortic ure stenosis (AS)."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"In late stages, stroke volume declines, leading to potential systolic pressure drop and narrowed pulse pressure."},{"k":"img","v":3478},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Carotid Pulse"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Characterized by a slow rise and delayed peak (pulsus parvus et tardus/Anacrotic pulse) A thrill or anacrotic “shudder” may be felt over the carotid arteries, often more prominent on the left"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"JVP Accentuated “a” wave due to reduced right ventricular"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"(RV) cavity distensibility from the hypertrophied interventricular septum."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"LV Impulse Lateral displacement in advanced stages."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"A double apical impulse and palpable S4 may be noted, especially in the left lateral recumbent position."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Auscultation findings:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"An early systolic ejection sound may be heard in younger patients with congenital bicuspid aortic valves, typically disappearing as the valve calcifies."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Severe AS may cause aortic valve closure to coincide with pulmonic valve closure, leading to paradoxical splitting of S2 (Initial stages - Single S2)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"S4 sound at the apex indicates LV hypertrophy"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"S3 appears in advanced stages when LV function declines."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Murmur: (Crescendo-Decrescendo Murmur) Ejection (mid) systolic murmur begins shortly after S1, increases in intensity, and ends before aortic valve closure. It is characterized as low-pitched and rough, typically loudest at the second right intercostal space. It can radiateto the carotid arteries and may be mistaken for mitral regurgitation due to the Gallavardineffect , where high-frequency components are perceived over the apex."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ejection (mid) systolic murmur begins shortly after S1, increases in intensity, and ends before aortic valve closure."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is characterized as low-pitched and rough, typically loudest at the second right intercostal space."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It can radiate to the carotid arteries and may be mistaken for mitral regurgitation due to the Gallavardin effect , where high-frequency components are perceived over the apex."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ejection (mid) systolic murmur begins shortly after S1, increases in intensity, and ends before aortic valve closure."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is characterized as low-pitched and rough, typically loudest at the second right intercostal space."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It can radiate to the carotid arteries and may be mistaken for mitral regurgitation due to the Gallavardin effect , where high-frequency components are perceived over the apex."},{"k":"img","v":3481},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Electrocardiography (ECG):"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"LV hypertrophy is common in severe AS."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Advanced cases may show ST-segment depression and T-wave inversion (LV strain), but these findings do not always correlate with hemodynamic severity."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Echocardiography:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It reveals thickened, calcified valve leaflets, reduced systolic opening, and LV hypertrophy."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Congenital bicuspid valves show eccentric closure."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Doppler measurements estimate valve gradient and area; Severe AS is defined by a valve area <1 cm²"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Right atrial enlargement (Option B) is not typical in isolated aortic stenosis but may occur in other conditions, such as tricuspid valve disease."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"ST-segment elevation (Option C) indicates acute myocardial infarction , which is not the primary finding in aortic stenosis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Atrial fibrillation (Option D) can occur in aortic stenosis but is uncommon as an initial finding and is more often associated with mitral valve involvement or significant atrial dilation can occur, but it is not a common initial finding in AS and is primarily associated with mitral valve involvement."}]},{"n":16,"q":"A 65-year-old patient presents to the clinic with increasing fatigue and shortness of breath on exertion. O/E a harsh, systolic crescendo-decrescendo ejection murmur is noted at the right second intercostal space. Which of the following symptoms is not part of the classic triad associated with this condition?","o":[{"t":"Exertional dyspnea"},{"t":"Angina pectoris"},{"t":"Syncope"},{"t":"Orthopnea"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The clinical scenario described indicates a likely case of Aortic stenosis , characterized by a harsh systolic crescendo-decrescendo ejection murmur typically heard at the right second intercostal space."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Orthopnea typically indicates HF/conditions related to fluid overload, but it is not one of the hallmark symptoms of aortic stenosis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Aortic Stenosis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Symptom onset: AS is clinically significant when the aortic valve orifice narrows to ~1 cm². Severe AS can remain asymptomatic for years due to LV compensation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"AS is clinically significant when the aortic valve orifice narrows to ~1 cm²."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Severe AS can remain asymptomatic for years due to LV compensation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Symptom progression: Often develops in the sixth to eighth decades; patients with bicuspid aortic valves may experience symptoms 10-20 years earlier."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Clinical Manifestations of Aortic Stenosis: The classic triad of symptoms: Exertional dyspnea (Option A ruled out) : Shortness of breath during physical activity due to elevated pulmonary capillary pressure from impaired left ventricular function and reduced cardiac output. Angina pectoris (Option B ruled out) : Chest pain occurs due to myocardial ischemia in aortic stenosis, resulting from increased oxygen demand and decreased supply. Syncope (Option C ruled out) : Loss of consciousness due to reduced cerebral perfusion, often occurring during exertion Early symptoms : Fatigue and reduced exercise tolerance. Advanced stages: Orthopnea (Option D) Paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea Pulmonary edema."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Late Findings: RV failure Systemic venous hypertension Hepatomegaly Atrial fibrillationTricuspid regurgitation"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The classic triad of symptoms: Exertional dyspnea (Option A ruled out) : Shortness of breath during physical activity due to elevated pulmonary capillary pressure from impaired left ventricular function and reduced cardiac output. Angina pectoris (Option B ruled out) : Chest pain occurs due to myocardial ischemia in aortic stenosis, resulting from increased oxygen demand and decreased supply. Syncope (Option C ruled out) : Loss of consciousness due to reduced cerebral perfusion, often occurring during exertion"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Exertional dyspnea (Option A ruled out) : Shortness of breath during physical activity due to elevated pulmonary capillary pressure from impaired left ventricular function and reduced cardiac output."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Angina pectoris (Option B ruled out) : Chest pain occurs due to myocardial ischemia in aortic stenosis, resulting from increased oxygen demand and decreased supply."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Syncope (Option C ruled out) : Loss of consciousness due to reduced cerebral perfusion, often occurring during exertion"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Early symptoms : Fatigue and reduced exercise tolerance."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Advanced stages: Orthopnea (Option D) Paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea Pulmonary edema."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Orthopnea (Option D)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pulmonary edema."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Late Findings: RV failure Systemic venous hypertension Hepatomegaly Atrial fibrillation Tricuspid regurgitation"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"RV failure"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Systemic venous hypertension"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hepatomegaly"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Atrial fibrillation"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Tricuspid regurgitation"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"AS is clinically significant when the aortic valve orifice narrows to ~1 cm²."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Severe AS can remain asymptomatic for years due to LV compensation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The classic triad of symptoms: Exertional dyspnea (Option A ruled out) : Shortness of breath during physical activity due to elevated pulmonary capillary pressure from impaired left ventricular function and reduced cardiac output. Angina pectoris (Option B ruled out) : Chest pain occurs due to myocardial ischemia in aortic stenosis, resulting from increased oxygen demand and decreased supply. Syncope (Option C ruled out) : Loss of consciousness due to reduced cerebral perfusion, often occurring during exertion"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Exertional dyspnea (Option A ruled out) : Shortness of breath during physical activity due to elevated pulmonary capillary pressure from impaired left ventricular function and reduced cardiac output."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Angina pectoris (Option B ruled out) : Chest pain occurs due to myocardial ischemia in aortic stenosis, resulting from increased oxygen demand and decreased supply."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Syncope (Option C ruled out) : Loss of consciousness due to reduced cerebral perfusion, often occurring during exertion"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Early symptoms : Fatigue and reduced exercise tolerance."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Advanced stages: Orthopnea (Option D) Paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea Pulmonary edema."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Orthopnea (Option D)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pulmonary edema."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Late Findings: RV failure Systemic venous hypertension Hepatomegaly Atrial fibrillation Tricuspid regurgitation"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"RV failure"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Systemic venous hypertension"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hepatomegaly"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Atrial fibrillation"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Tricuspid regurgitation"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The classic triad of symptoms:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Exertional dyspnea (Option A ruled out) : Shortness of breath during physical activity due to elevated pulmonary capillary pressure from impaired left ventricular function and reduced cardiac output."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Angina pectoris (Option B ruled out) : Chest pain occurs due to myocardial ischemia in aortic stenosis, resulting from increased oxygen demand and decreased supply."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Syncope (Option C ruled out) : Loss of consciousness due to reduced cerebral perfusion, often occurring during exertion"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Orthopnea (Option D)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pulmonary edema."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"RV failure"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Systemic venous hypertension"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hepatomegaly"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Atrial fibrillation"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Tricuspid regurgitation"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Interactions with Other Conditions: Mitral stenosis can mask AS symptoms by lowering cardiac output, while aortic regurgitation may increase the transaortic pressure gradient due to higher flow rates."}]},{"n":17,"q":"A 72-year-old woman presents with fatigue and progressive shortness of breath, particularly when climbing stairs. During the physical examination, a harsh, systolic ejection murmur is heard at the right second intercostal space, radiating to the carotids. Which of the following factors is least likely to be associated with the development of the condition?","o":[{"t":"Mediastinal irradiation"},{"t":"Mutation in NOTCH1"},{"t":"Prior rheumatic inflammation"},{"t":"Systemic lupus erythematosus"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The given clinical scenario with systolic ejection murmur heard at the right second intercostal space radiating to the carotids suggests Aortic Stenosis ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"While mediastinal irradiation, mutation in NOTCH1, and prior rheumatic inflammation are all factors associated with aortic valve dysfunction."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Systemic lupus erythematosus is less directly linked to the development of Aortic Stenosis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Etiology of Aortic stenosis (AS):"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Degenerative Calcification The primary cause of aortic stenosis (AS) in adults,"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"often due to: Congenital Bicuspid Aortic Valve (BAV) : Approximately 53% of cases. Trileaflet Valve Deterioration Previous Rheumatic Fever (Option C ruled out) : Inflammation can lead to scarring and calcification."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Rheumatic Heart Disease It causes commissural fusion, resembling BAV, and"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"leads to valve narrowing."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Mediastinal Radiation (Option Cause late scarring and calcification of the aortic leaflets. A ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Shone Complex Components include Aortic stenosis , Coarctation of"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"aorta, Parachute mitral valve, supravalvular mitral membrane"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Genetic polymorphisms Vitamin D receptor"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Estrogen receptor (notably in postmenopausal women) Interleukin 10 Apolipoprotein E4"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Traditional risk factors Hypertension, High LDL, Smoking, DM, CKD Degenerative Calcification The primary cause of aortic stenosis (AS) in adults,"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"often due to: Congenital Bicuspid Aortic Valve (BAV) : Approximately 53% of cases. Trileaflet Valve Deterioration Previous Rheumatic Fever (Option C ruled out) : Inflammation can lead to scarring and calcification."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Bicuspid Aortic Valve Disease Most common congenital heart valve defect, with a male"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"predominance (2–4:1) . Inheritance : Autosomal dominant with incomplete penetrance Genetic Factors : Mutations in NOTCH1 (Option B ruled out) , GATA5 , and GATA4 have been noted"},{"k":"img","v":3488}]},{"n":18,"q":"A 65-year-old male presents with exertional dyspnea and a prominent heart murmur. Upon auscultation, the murmur is best heard at the right second intercostal space and radiates to the carotids. Interestingly, the murmur also becomes higher-pitched when heard at the apex. What is the most likely diagnosis associated with this auscultatory finding?","o":[{"t":"Mitral regurgitation"},{"t":"Aortic stenosis"},{"t":"Tricuspid regurgitation"},{"t":"Pulmonary stenosis"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The given clinical presentation explains the Gallavardin effect and it is associated with Aortic stenosis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Gallavardin Effect:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It refers to the phenomenon where a murmur of aortic stenosis (AS) becomes higher-pitched and more acoustically pure when auscultated at the apex of the heart despite originating from the aortic region."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The ejection (mid) systolic (Crescendo-decrescendo) murmur of AS typically starts shortly after S1, increases in intensity, peaks during ejection, and ends just before aortic valve closure."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"murmur of AS\" data-author=\"\" data-hash=\"8860\" data-license=\"\" data-source=\"\" data-tags=\"April2025\" height=\"564\" src=\"https://image.prepladder.com/notes/qVQTMDsICPOgG 9vZx3oX1744310589.png\" width=\"564\" />"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is usually low-pitched, rough, and loudest at the base of the heart , particularly in the second right intercostal space."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The murmur is transmitted along the carotid arteries, which is a classic sign of AS"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"When the murmur is auscultated at the apex, it may appear higher-pitched and can sometimes be confused with the systolic murmur of mitral regurgitation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Clinical Implication:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The Gallavardin effect is important for differentiating between AS and other conditions like mitral regurgitation, as the change in pitch can lead to diagnostic confusion."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Mitral regurgitation (Option A) typically presents with a holosystolic murmur best heard at the apex radiating to the axilla, not the carotids. It is unrelated to the right second intercostal space."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Tricuspid regurgitation (Option C) produces a holosystolic murmur at the lower left sternal border that increases with inspiration and does not typically radiate to the carotids."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Pulmonary stenosis (Option D) causes a systolic murmur best heard at the left upper sternal border, often radiating to the left shoulder or back."}]},{"n":19,"q":"A 60-year-old female patient presents with exertional dyspnea and a history of rheumatic fever. An echocardiogram shows isolated mitral stenosis with a calcified valve. Which of the following is the most appropriate management strategy for her condition?","o":[{"t":"Initiate warfarin therapy"},{"t":"Percutaneous mitral balloon commissurotomy (PMBC)"},{"t":"Schedule for mitral valve replacement"},{"t":"Start beta-blockers and diuretics"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Mitral valve replacement is indicated for significant symptoms in patients with mitral stenosis and a calcified valve, particularly when heavy calcification makes the valve unsuitable for balloon valvuloplasty."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Management of Mitral Stenosis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Medical Management: Patients with mild symptoms may be managed medically. Penicillin Prophylaxis : Essential for preventing rheumatic fever in risk patients. Symptomatic Relief : Sodium restriction, diuretics, beta-blockers (Option D ruled out), and Calcium channel blockers help manage symptoms and control heart rate in atrial fibrillation (AF)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Anticoagulation : Warfarin for patients with AF, history of thromboembolism, or left atrialthrombus; INR target 2–3. (Option A ruled out) Cardioversion : For recent-onset AF, ensureanticoagulation for at least three weeks. Infective Endocarditis Prophylaxis : Routineantibiotic prophylaxis is no longer recommended."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Penicillin Prophylaxis : Essential for preventing rheumatic fever in risk patients."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Symptomatic Relief : Sodium restriction, diuretics, beta-blockers (Option D ruled out), and Calcium channel blockers help manage symptoms and control heart rate in atrial fibrillation (AF)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Anticoagulation : Warfarin for patients with AF, history of thromboembolism, or left atrial thrombus; INR target 2–3. (Option A ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cardioversion : For recent-onset AF, ensure anticoagulation for at least three weeks."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Infective Endocarditis Prophylaxis : Routine antibiotic prophylaxis is no longer recommended."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Interventional: Considered if patients remain symptomatic despite medical management or if pulmonary hypertension develops/Severe MS (<1.5 cm²) Balloon valvuloplasty Mitral valvotomy/Mitral valve replacement"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Balloon valvuloplasty"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Mitral valvotomy/Mitral valve replacement"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Penicillin Prophylaxis : Essential for preventing rheumatic fever in risk patients."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Symptomatic Relief : Sodium restriction, diuretics, beta-blockers (Option D ruled out), and Calcium channel blockers help manage symptoms and control heart rate in atrial fibrillation (AF)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Anticoagulation : Warfarin for patients with AF, history of thromboembolism, or left atrial thrombus; INR target 2–3. (Option A ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cardioversion : For recent-onset AF, ensure anticoagulation for at least three weeks."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Infective Endocarditis Prophylaxis : Routine antibiotic prophylaxis is no longer recommended."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Balloon valvuloplasty"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Mitral valvotomy/Mitral valve replacement"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Procedure Indications / Requirements"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Mitral Balloon Valvuloplasty (PMBC) (Option B Significant symptoms present ruled out) Isolated mitral stenosis (no or trivial mitral regurgitation)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Mobile, non-calcified valve/sub valve apparatus on echocardiogram Left atrium free of thrombus"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Procedure Indications / Requirements"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Surgical Commissurotomy Required if PMBC fails"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Involves opening valve commissures and addressing subvalvular structures"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Mitral Valve Replacement (Option C) Substantial mitral regurgitation"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Rigid and calcified valve Presence of thrombus formation"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Mitral Balloon Valvuloplasty/Percutaneous Mitral Balloon Commissurotomy:"},{"k":"img","v":3493}]},{"n":20,"q":"A 48-year-old female presents with worsening exertional dyspnea and fatigue. She has a history of rheumatic fever during her childhood. O/E, you note a diastolic murmur at the cardiac apex, along with a pronounced opening snap. Which of the following findings is least likely to be present?","o":[{"t":"Left atrial enlargement"},{"t":"Reduced mitral valve area"},{"t":"Right ventricular hypertrophy"},{"t":"Mitral regurgitation"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The clinical scenario of worsening exertional dyspnea, a history of rheumatic fever, and the presence of a diastolic murmur with an opening snap strongly suggests Mitral stenosis & the primary issue is obstruction, leading to left atrial enlargement and a reduced mitral valve area."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Mitral regurgitation is less likely unless there is additional valve pathology."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Mitral Stenosis (MS):"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Aetiology: Primary Cause : Rheumatic fever is the leading cause of mitral stenosis, accounting for about 40% of cases in patients with a history of rheumatic heart disease."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Congenital abnormalities (e.g., parachute valve, cor triatriatum) Mitral annular calcificationwith involvement of leaflets Autoimmune diseases (e.g., systemic lupus erythematosus,rheumatoid arthritis) Left atrial myxoma Infective endocarditis with large vegetation"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Primary Cause : Rheumatic fever is the leading cause of mitral stenosis, accounting for about 40% of cases in patients with a history of rheumatic heart disease."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Congenital abnormalities (e.g., parachute valve, cor triatriatum)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Mitral annular calcification with involvement of leaflets"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Autoimmune diseases (e.g., systemic lupus erythematosus, rheumatoid arthritis)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Left atrial myxoma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Infective endocarditis with large vegetation"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Primary Cause : Rheumatic fever is the leading cause of mitral stenosis, accounting for about 40% of cases in patients with a history of rheumatic heart disease."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Congenital abnormalities (e.g., parachute valve, cor triatriatum)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Mitral annular calcification with involvement of leaflets"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Autoimmune diseases (e.g., systemic lupus erythematosus, rheumatoid arthritis)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Left atrial myxoma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Infective endocarditis with large vegetation"},{"k":"img","v":3496},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pathology : Chronic inflammation leads to thickening of the valve leaflets, fibrous tissue formation, calcific deposits, and fusion of the mitral commissures and chordae tendinae. This process results in a narrowed, funnel-shaped valve. (Fish-mouth Appearance) Normal mitral valve area : 4–6 cm²; significant obstruction if <2 cm². (Option B ruled out) Hemodynamics : In severe MS (<1.5 cm²), a left atrial (LA) pressure of ~25 mmHg is needed to maintain normal cardiac output (CO). Effects of Heart Rate : Increased heart rate diminishes diastolic filling time, raising the LA pressure and transvalvular gradient"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Normal mitral valve area : 4–6 cm²; significant obstruction if <2 cm². (Option B ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hemodynamics : In severe MS (<1.5 cm²), a left atrial (LA) pressure of ~25 mmHg is needed to maintain normal cardiac output (CO)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Effects of Heart Rate : Increased heart rate diminishes diastolic filling time, raising the LA pressure and transvalvular gradient"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Symptoms : Exertional dyspnea, cough, and progressive limitation of daily activities. Orthopnea and paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea can develop as MS worsens. Atrial fibrillation (AF) often accelerates symptom progression."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Exertional dyspnea, cough, and progressive limitation of daily activities."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Orthopnea and paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea can develop as MS worsens."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Atrial fibrillation (AF) often accelerates symptom progression."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Complications: Pulmonary Hypertension (Leading to RVH & RHF) (Option C ruled out) Atrial Fibrillation (Due to Left atrial enlargement) Thrombus Formation: Stasis of blood in the enlarged left atrium, increases the risk of thrombus formation, especially in patients with atrial fibrillation. Hemoptysis (Elevated pulmonary venous pressure - rupture of small veins) Infective Endocarditis Pulmonary Infections"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pulmonary Hypertension (Leading to RVH & RHF) (Option C ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Atrial Fibrillation (Due to Left atrial enlargement)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Thrombus Formation: Stasis of blood in the enlarged left atrium, increases the risk of thrombus formation, especially in patients with atrial fibrillation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hemoptysis (Elevated pulmonary venous pressure - rupture of small veins)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Infective Endocarditis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pulmonary Infections"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Findings: Inspection : Malar flush, jugular venous distension, and right ventricular (RV) enlargement. Auscultation : A loud S1, an opening snap (OS) after S2, and a low-pitched, rumbling mid-diastolic murmur with presystolic accentuation- heard best at the apex. Characteristics of a murmur in severe MS : Decreased interval between A2 & OS. Wide splitting of S2. Palpable systolic thrill. The duration of the murmur increases."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Inspection : Malar flush, jugular venous distension, and right ventricular (RV) enlargement."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Auscultation : A loud S1, an opening snap (OS) after S2, and a low-pitched, rumbling mid-diastolic murmur with presystolic accentuation- heard best at the apex. Characteristics of a murmur in severe MS : Decreased interval between A2 & OS. Wide splitting of S2. Palpable systolic thrill. The duration of the murmur increases."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Characteristics of a murmur in severe MS : Decreased interval between A2 & OS. Wide splitting of S2. Palpable systolic thrill. The duration of the murmur increases."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Decreased interval between A2 & OS."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Wide splitting of S2."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Palpable systolic thrill."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The duration of the murmur increases."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Normal mitral valve area : 4–6 cm²; significant obstruction if <2 cm². (Option B ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hemodynamics : In severe MS (<1.5 cm²), a left atrial (LA) pressure of ~25 mmHg is needed to maintain normal cardiac output (CO)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Effects of Heart Rate : Increased heart rate diminishes diastolic filling time, raising the LA pressure and transvalvular gradient"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Exertional dyspnea, cough, and progressive limitation of daily activities."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Orthopnea and paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea can develop as MS worsens."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Atrial fibrillation (AF) often accelerates symptom progression."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pulmonary Hypertension (Leading to RVH & RHF) (Option C ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Atrial Fibrillation (Due to Left atrial enlargement)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Thrombus Formation: Stasis of blood in the enlarged left atrium, increases the risk of thrombus formation, especially in patients with atrial fibrillation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hemoptysis (Elevated pulmonary venous pressure - rupture of small veins)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Infective Endocarditis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pulmonary Infections"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Inspection : Malar flush, jugular venous distension, and right ventricular (RV) enlargement."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Auscultation : A loud S1, an opening snap (OS) after S2, and a low-pitched, rumbling mid-diastolic murmur with presystolic accentuation- heard best at the apex. Characteristics of a murmur in severe MS : Decreased interval between A2 & OS. Wide splitting of S2. Palpable systolic thrill. The duration of the murmur increases."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Characteristics of a murmur in severe MS : Decreased interval between A2 & OS. Wide splitting of S2. Palpable systolic thrill. The duration of the murmur increases."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Decreased interval between A2 & OS."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Wide splitting of S2."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Palpable systolic thrill."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The duration of the murmur increases."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Characteristics of a murmur in severe MS : Decreased interval between A2 & OS. Wide splitting of S2. Palpable systolic thrill. The duration of the murmur increases."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Decreased interval between A2 & OS."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Wide splitting of S2."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Palpable systolic thrill."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The duration of the murmur increases."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Decreased interval between A2 & OS."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Wide splitting of S2."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Palpable systolic thrill."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The duration of the murmur increases."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"murmur in severe MS\" data-author=\"\" data-hash=\"8856\" data-license=\"\" data-source=\"\" data-tags=\"April2025\" height=\"532\" src=\"https://image.prepladder.com/notes/Ch2RCevxGFGKiw 3DvKhT1744310319.png\" width=\"532\" />"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Investigations: ECG : Often shows LA enlargement (Option A ruled out) , right axis deviation, and RV hypertrophy. Echocardiogram : Measures mitral inflow velocity and transvalvular gradients and evaluates for associated mitral regurgitation (MR) or other valvular lesions. Chest X-ray : Shows LA enlargement, pulmonary vein dilation, and Kerley B lines indicating pulmonary congestion."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"ECG : Often shows LA enlargement (Option A ruled out) , right axis deviation, and RV hypertrophy."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Echocardiogram : Measures mitral inflow velocity and transvalvular gradients and evaluates for associated mitral regurgitation (MR) or other valvular lesions."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Chest X-ray : Shows LA enlargement, pulmonary vein dilation, and Kerley B lines indicating pulmonary congestion."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"ECG : Often shows LA enlargement (Option A ruled out) , right axis deviation, and RV hypertrophy."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Echocardiogram : Measures mitral inflow velocity and transvalvular gradients and evaluates for associated mitral regurgitation (MR) or other valvular lesions."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Chest X-ray : Shows LA enlargement, pulmonary vein dilation, and Kerley B lines indicating pulmonary congestion."}]},{"n":21,"q":"A 62-year-old female with a history of rheumatic heart disease comes to the OPD for a regular follow-up. Echocardiography confirmed the presence of severe mitral regurgitation (MR). When considering her treatment options, which of the following interventions should not be included in her management plan?","o":[{"t":"Use of Apixaban if her CHA2DS2-VASc score ‡2"},{"t":"Cardiac resynchronization therapy, when indicated"},{"t":"Mitral valve repair if ejection fraction falls less than 60%"},{"t":"Vitamin K antagonists"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The CHAnDSn-VASc score is a clinical prediction tool used to estimate the risk of stroke in patients with atrial fibrillation (AF), and if a patient has a CHA2DS2-VASc score ‡2, it means that he is at a high risk of stroke and anticoagulation is recommended."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In patients with RHD, especially those with mitral valve disease, Vitamin K antagonists (VKAs) like warfarin are the preferred anticoagulants for preventing thromboembolic events, particularly if atrial fibrillation is present."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Non-vitamin K oral anticoagulants (NOACs) like apixaban are generally not recommended for patients with rheumatic mitral valve disease, as these agents have not been well studied or proven to be safe and effective in this population."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Management of Mitral Regurgitation"},{"k":"img","v":3505},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Investigations"},{"k":"img","v":3510},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"ECG Atrial fibrillation may be present in cases of chronic MR P-mitrale > 120 seconds (enlarged left atrium) Echocardiogram Regurgitant flow of blood from the left ventricle to the left atrium Chest X-ray Double atrial shadow (left atrium enlarged) Walking man sign (› carinal angle) Signs of pulmonary oedema: Pulmonary congestion and Kerley B lines. Left ventricular dilatation due to volume overload (MS, there is compensatory hypertrophy)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Management of Mitral Regurgitation"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Treatment Acute MR leading to decompensated heart failure can"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"be treated with: Loop diuretics Sodium nitroprusside Mechanical support till surgical repair Chronic MR For secondary MR, aggressive guideline-directed therapy (GDMT) can be done using. Beta-blockers (Atenolol and Metoprolol) ACE inhibitors (Lisinopril and Captopril) Angiotensin receptor blockers (Telmisartan and Losartan) Cardiac resynchronization therapy for arrhythmias (Option B ruled out) Warfarin or NOAC if atrial fibrillation occurs NOAC should be avoided in the presence of rheumatic heart disease and mechanical prosthetic valves (Option A) Warfarin is preferred in RHD with valvular atrial fibrillation (Option D ruled out) Surgical repair: Mitral valve repair > replacement Transcatheter mitral valve repair (Mitra clip) Surgical repair in asymptomatic patients must be done when EF<60% or LV ESD >= 40mm. (Option C ruled out)"}]},{"n":22,"q":"A 65-year-old female presents to the clinic with complaints of fatigue, shortness of breath on exertion, and palpitations. On physical examination, a systolic thrill is palpable at the cardiac apex. Echocardiography showed severe backflow of blood from the left ventricle to the left atrium during ventricular systole. What kind of murmur is most likely to be heard during cardiac auscultation of this patient?","o":[{"t":"Continuous murmur"},{"t":"An opening snap followed by a diastolic murmur"},{"t":"Holosystolic murmur, best heard at the apex"},{"t":"Crescendo decrescendo systolic murmur best heard at the base"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The clinical presentation and echocardiography show features of severe mitral regurgitation , so on cardiac auscultation, a holosystolic murmur will most likely be heard at the cardiac apex."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"MITRAL REGURGIT ATION"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Clinical examination BP may be normal or fl ; Narrow pulse pressure."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Palpable thrill at the cardiac apex. Hyperdynamic and laterally displaced apex beat. Normal or › JVP."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"MITRAL REGURGIT ATION"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Auscultation findings Soft or absent S1."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Wide variable splitting of S2: P2 is variable, and A2 appears early as less blood flows through the aortic valve; thus, the S2 split is wide. S4 may be heard in acute severe MR."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Murmur Holosystolic murmur in chronic MR . (High-pitched"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"blowing murmur, radiating towards axilla) A decrescendo early systolic murmur in acute severe MR."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Continuous murmurs (Option A) occur during both systole and diastole, which is not characteristic of mitral regurgitation. They are typically associated with conditions that involve persistent blood flow between high- and low-pressure areas, such as in patent ductus arteriosus (PDA), where there is a constant flow of blood between the aorta and pulmonary artery."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"An opening snap followed by a diastolic murmur (Option B) is heard in mitral stenosis . It occurs due to the narrowed mitral valve opening during diastole, resulting in an opening snap followed by a low-pitched diastolic rumble."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Crescendo-decrescendo systolic murmur (Option C) is typical of aortic stenosis . It is heard best at the base of the heart and radiates to the carotids. This murmur reflects the turbulent flow of blood through the narrowing of the aortic valve."}]},{"n":23,"q":"A 55-year-old male presents with shortness of breath on exertion, fatigue, and palpitations. On examination, a pan systolic murmur is heard best at the apex, radiating to the axilla. Echocardiography reveals mitral valve prolapse with significant mitral regurgitation. Which of the following is NOT a typical cause of primary chronic mitral regurgitation?","o":[{"t":"Ischemic cardiomyopathy"},{"t":"Rheumatic fever"},{"t":"Infective endocarditis"},{"t":"Radiation"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Primary chronic mitral regurgitation (MR) refers to structural abnormalities of the mitral valve itself. Ischemic cardiomyopathy, however, typically causes secondary (functional) mitral regurgitation due to left ventricular dilation or dysfunction, not a primary structural issue of the valve."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"MITRAL REGURGIT ATION"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Etiology Mitral Regurgitation \" data-author=\"\" data-hash=\"8849\" (Options B, C & D data-license=\"\" data-source=\"\" data-tags=\"April2025\" ruled out) height=\"233\" src=\"https://image.prepladder.com/notes/g"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"SmFC0gAlY0S8WIJwrTM1744309732.png\" width=\"450\" />"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Pathophysiology Mitral Regurgitation \" data-author=\"\" data-hash=\"8850\""},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"data-license=\"\" data-source=\"\" data-tags=\"April2025\" height=\"195\" src=\"https://image.prepladder.com/notes/b3 CWUiFdP7NreV9jpcCk1744309819.png\" width=\"421\" />"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Clinical features Mild to moderate MR is asymptomatic"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Severe MR Orthopnea Exertional fatigue Paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea Ankle oedema Late-onset right-sided heart failure may also occur"}]},{"n":24,"q":"A 72-year-old male with mitral valve prolapse, chronic atrial fibrillation (CHA2DS2-VASc score of 5), severe mitral regurgitation, and an ejection fraction of 35% presents with occasional palpitations and breathlessness. He also has a history of infective endocarditis. Which of the following statements regarding his management is not true?","o":[{"t":"Anticoagulation therapy should be avoided"},{"t":"Beta-blockers should be initiated for his palpitations"},{"t":"Mitral valve repair is preferred over mitral valve replacement"},{"t":"Prophylactic antibiotics should be initiated"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"As the patient has a high CHA2DS2-VASc score, anticoagulation therapy should be initiated to reduce the risk of cerebrovascular accident ."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"CHA2DS2-VASc: The CHA2DS2-VASc score is a clinical tool used to estimate the risk of stroke in patients with atrial fibrillation (AF) and to guide anticoagulation therapy."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"C ongestive heart failure (1 point)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"H ypertension (1 point)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"A ge ‡ 75 years (2 points)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"D iabetes mellitus (1 point)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"S troke/TIA/TE history (2 points)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"V ascular disease (1 point)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"A ge 65-74 years (1 point)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"S ex category (female sex, 1 point)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Scoring:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"0 points : Low risk (consider no anticoagulation)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"1 point : Moderate risk (consider anticoagulation, especially if female)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"2 or more points : High risk (anticoagulation recommended)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Investigations"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"ECG Biphasic or inverted T waves in lead II, III and aVF Occasional Supraventricular tachycardia and Premature Ventricular Contraction may be seen"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Biphasic or inverted T waves in lead II, III and aVF"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Occasional Supraventricular tachycardia and Premature Ventricular Contraction may be seen"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Biphasic or inverted T waves in lead II, III and aVF"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Occasional Supraventricular tachycardia and Premature Ventricular Contraction may be seen"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Transthoracic echocardiography (parasternal long axis view) Bulge > 2mm above mitral annulus (belly of mitral valve leaflet)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Bulge > 2mm above mitral annulus (belly of mitral valve leaflet)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Bulge > 2mm above mitral annulus (belly of mitral valve leaflet)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Transesophageal echocardiography Indicated when more accurate anatomic information is required. Done during surgery for intraoperative guidance during valvular repair."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Indicated when more accurate anatomic information is required."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Done during surgery for intraoperative guidance during valvular repair."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Indicated when more accurate anatomic information is required."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Done during surgery for intraoperative guidance during valvular repair."},{"k":"img","v":3517},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Management:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Palpitations: Beta Blockers (Option B ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Atrial fibrillation/prevention of stroke (Option A) Anticoagulants should be initiated if the CHA2DS2-VASc score is more than or equal to 2."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Anticoagulants should be initiated if the CHA2DS2-VASc score is more than or equal to 2."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Transient ischemic attack (mild): Low-dose aspirin"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Endocarditis prophylaxis (Option D ruled out): Antibiotics are indicated if a history of endocarditis is present."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Surgical repair (Option C ruled out): Transcatheter valvular repair is preferred over replacement (Repair preserves the native valve, improves outcomes, and decreases postoperative complications)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Anticoagulants should be initiated if the CHA2DS2-VASc score is more than or equal to 2."}]}]);
